he landlord started low, I should not let them keep initiative.  \n\nReason:  \nPlaying a mid-range single (♠J) beats the landlord’s ♥3 and pressures them to use a higher card without committing my strongest assets (RedJoker, ♥2, ♦A). This maintains some control and allows my teammate to potentially save their higher cards. If I play too low, the landlord might easily beat it with a low card and retain initiative. If I pass, the landlord’s weak play stands, and they might follow with a stronger combination, putting us on the defensive. Thus, ♠J is a balanced choice.  \n\nAnswer:  \n{"cards": ["♠J"]}'}
